Abstract

This study examined whether speech-based interfaces for different in-vehicle-information-systems (IVIS) reduce the distraction caused by these systems. For three frequently used systems (audio, telephone with name selection, navigation system with address entry and point of interest selection) speech , manual control and driving without IVIS (baseline) were compared. The Lane Change Task was used to assess driving performance. Additionally, gaze behavior and a subjective measure of distraction were analyzed. Speech interfaces improved driving performance, gaze behavior and subjective distraction for all systems with the exception of point-of-interest entry. However, these improvements were overall not strong enough to reach the baseline performance level. Only in easy segments of the driving task the performance level was comparable to baseline. Thus, speech-based IVIS have to be further developed to keep the cognitive complexity at an adequate level which does not disturb driving. However, looking at the benefits, speech control is a must for the car of the future.
Introduction

Distraction of the driver is one of the major causes for car accidents. The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (Wang et al., 1996, Ranney et al., 2000) estimates that approximately 25% of the accidents in the years 1995–1999 which were registered by the police were due to inattention of the driver. Further analysis identified some specific sources of distraction which were “adjusting the radio, cassette or CD” comprising 11.4% of the crashes, “other device/object in the vehicle” with 2.9% and “using a cell phone” (e.g. listening, talking and dialing) with 1.5% (Stutts et al., 2001). These results were based on self-reports of drivers with accidents. Thus, one may assume that these numbers underestimate the real scope of the problem. This assumption is supported by the real driving data analysis of Klauer et al. (2006) using the “100 car naturalistic driving study”. It was found that in 40% of all drives secondary tasks were conducted. Additionally, more and more systems and functions have become available in the car, e.g. playing CDs and DVDs, phoning, entering a destination in the navigation system, etc. This rapid development of increasingly sophisticated information systems is associated with more complex menu structures which add to the mental workload of the driver and which will probably further increase distraction.

The effects of engaging in these kinds of secondary tasks have been demonstrated in two simulator studies. Chisholm et al. (2008) found a significant increase of reaction time to several hazards (pedestrian walking into the path of car; parked vehicle pulling out of the side of the roadway; leading car braking suddenly) while selecting a specific song in an MP3 player. Furthermore, the number of glances and the glance duration away from the road were increased in a difficult MP3 player condition. Reed and Robbins (2008) conducted a simulator study to investigate the impact of text messaging while driving. Results show that participants’ driving behavior was impaired, particularly reaction time (35% slower when writing a text message) and the ability to maintain lateral vehicle control. While texting, participants slowed down significantly, indicating that they were aware of the distraction and potential risk and tried to compensate for it. Apparently, these compensatory efforts were not successful.

These effects may be explained by the fact that humans have only a limited amount of available cognitive resources at any given time. However, according to the multiple resource theory of attention (Wickens, 2002) there are different pools of resources which can be tapped in parallel. These pools concern on the one hand input modalities (especially visual and acoustic input) and on the other hand reactions (manual vs. verbal reaction). Tasks which require the same resources will interfere. For example, operating a device via touch screen will interfere with driving a vehicle. From multiple resource theory, speech-based human–machine-interaction should be much better suited while driving because this taps different resources than driving.

However, a large number of studies have demonstrated that speaking and listening may also impair driving (for an overview, see Horrey and Wickens, 2006). For example, Lee et al. (2001) investigated driving performance while operating a speech-based e-mail system. Results show a 30% increase in reaction time to a braking lead vehicle when the speech-based task was carried out. Furthermore, subjective workload increased significantly while performing the e-mail task compared to a baseline condition. A follow-up study of Jameson et al. (2004) confirmed these results and demonstrated a significant reduction of the time to collision in the distraction condition.

These findings do not only apply to human–machine-interaction by ways of speech, but also for human communication. In a meta-analysis of the effects of cell phone use on driving performance, Caird et al. (2008) included 33 studies through 2007. Results show a decrease of reaction time to events and stimuli while talking, independent of the research setting (laboratory, driving simulation or on-road driving). Furthermore, there were no differences for using handheld or hands-free speaking systems. The mean increase in reaction time was found to be 0.25 s for all types of phone-related tasks. No significant difference was found between different communication modes (talking to a fellow passenger vs. talking via phone) or types (naturalistic vs. information-processing speaking tasks).

Although human communication and speech interfaces can have a negative impact on driving safety, speech interfaces may still be better than displays and manual controls. Engaging in a conversation and operating a device via speech apparently use resources which are also required for driving. This interference might be minimized by reducing the requirements for visual input and manual control exerted by the secondary task. In order to examine this idea, a direct comparison of the effect of device control via speech and via display and manual control during car driving is required. This is the aim of the study presented here.

Using a simple driving simulation (the Lane Change Task; Mattes, 2003) four commercially available in-vehicle information systems (two different navigation devices, a hands-free mobile phone car kit, a music system) were compared operating these either by a touch screen or a speech interface. To give a meaningful estimation of the effect size both conditions were compared to a baseline. The two navigation systems differed in the way the speech interface was designed enabling us to also obtain some results on the importance of interface design to driving safety.
