Results

The aim of the study is to examine whether speech operation while driving reduces the distraction as compared to manual-visual operation. To this aim, a 4 × 2 (four IVIS × two modes of operation) repeated measures MANOVA was computed including SDLP, reaction time, mean deviation, subjective distraction and percentage gaze away from the driving task. Both main effects and the interaction were highly significant (every p  = 0.001; IVIS: F (15,246)  = 7.4, η p 2 = 0.31 ; mode of operation: F (5,24)  = 80.5, η p 2 = 0.94 ;
Discussion

This study examined whether speech interfaces for different IVIS systems used in the car reduce the distraction that these systems are known to produce. Several typical systems (audio, phone number selection, navigation system with address entry and point-of-interest selection) were compared with speech and manual control, each. In order to measure driving performance, the Lane Change Task was used. Additionally, gaze behavior and a subjective measure of distraction were examined.

